WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.333
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.333 --> 00:00:10.878
It was 2015, we were three months
into our parenting journey,

00:00:10.878 --> 00:00:13.892
and there we were, new baby in arms,

00:00:13.892 --> 00:00:18.680
discussing a big topic that
would change our family life.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.380
My husband and I were talking about what
it would take to go from being an English

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:28.220
speaking household if we committed
to becoming a Spanish speaking one.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:32.200
To give you a little bit of context,
my husband and

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.840
I share a similar heritage background.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:39.810
We're both Mexican-Nicaraguan-American but
we're different generations.

00:00:39.810 --> 00:00:43.780
For me, my parents had been
the immigrants to this country.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.130
I'd been born in San Francisco and
raised in a Spanish speaking home.

00:00:48.130 --> 00:00:53.190
But for my husband, his grandparents had
been the immigrants to this country.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:58.115
His parents had been born in Los Angeles,
and raised at a time when they were

00:00:58.115 --> 00:01:01.089
beaten in school if they were heard
speaking a language other than English.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:04.480
In large part,
the policy had its intended effect.

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.382
My in-laws lost their Spanish and

00:01:07.382 --> 00:01:10.920
eventually raised my husband in
an English speaking household.

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:16.080
Fast forwarding back to our conversation
that day, my husband said, but

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.290
I'm an adult.

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:20.080
Aren't I too old to
learn another language?

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.810
And what about all those
things about American culture,

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:24.700
the things that I grew up with?

00:01:24.700 --> 00:01:26.740
If we choose to speak Spanish at home,

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.450
will I be able to share
those with my kids?

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:33.280
I reminded my husband that a choice to
speak Spanish at home didn't mean that our

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:35.390
kids wouldn't learn English.

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:36.680
They would learn English and

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:41.000
we wanted them to as choosing to
speak Spanish at home was more about

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.790
choosing to value both languages for
our kids and not one over the other.

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.370
I reminded my husband of all the times
that he'd shared with me how he'd

00:01:50.370 --> 00:01:52.800
regretted not knowing Spanish,

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.300
how it meant he didn't have the closest
relationship with his grandparents.

00:01:57.690 --> 00:01:58.887
I asked my husband,

00:01:58.887 --> 00:02:03.748
if we tell our kids it's really important
that you learn multiple languages, but

00:02:03.748 --> 00:02:08.126
we didn't find use for bilingualism
in our own lives, would they buy it?

00:02:08.126 --> 00:02:09.740
Would they think we were hypocrites?

00:02:11.410 --> 00:02:13.750
There are two things that
I could talk about today,

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:17.470
why Americans should be more bilingual or
how we become it.

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:21.620
Now given that we're in an academic
setting, I'm going to assume that many of

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:26.140
you have already heard about the benefits
of bilingualism, how bilingualism has

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:31.080
been linked to improved learning outcomes,
enhanced cognitive abilities, more

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:36.090
financial opportunity, the development
of empathy and interpretive skills.

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:39.260
Many of you might know that bilingualism
has been linked to delays in

00:02:39.260 --> 00:02:42.150
manifestations of aging,
like dementia and Alzheimer's.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:48.138
Given that this is Stanford after all,
some of you may even know that in 2016,

00:02:48.138 --> 00:02:53.106
the American Academy of Arts and Sciences
released a report that said that fluency

00:02:53.106 --> 00:02:58.074
in English is no longer sufficient to meet
the needs of America in a shrinking world

00:02:58.074 --> 00:03:02.754
nor the needs of individual citizens who
will interact with other peoples and

00:03:02.754 --> 00:03:06.079
cultures more than at any
other time in human history.

00:03:07.889 --> 00:03:12.591
The overwhelming recommendation of
the commission was that Americans learn

00:03:12.591 --> 00:03:16.857
to value language education the same
way we value education in math and

00:03:16.857 --> 00:03:19.330
English as a persistent national need.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:25.820
So instead of talking about why
America should become more bilingual,

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:27.860
I want to devote the rest of
my time to talking about how.

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:34.116
How do we go from our current state,
where 80% of Americans are monolingual and

00:03:34.116 --> 00:03:38.052
start inching towards becoming
more of a bilingual society?

00:03:38.052 --> 00:03:41.448
I'm going to suggest we can take three
immediate actions to move us in that

00:03:41.448 --> 00:03:42.870
direction.

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:47.680
First, businesses need to recognize
the incredible opportunity in this space.

00:03:48.720 --> 00:03:51.470
When my husband and
I decided to speak Spanish at home,

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:55.110
we were shocked at how few resources
we had to support our decision.

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:59.583
Things like an alphabet puzzle where
the letter corresponded to the word in

00:03:59.583 --> 00:04:04.275
Spanish or books that had a rich depth
of vocabulary, we couldn't find them.

00:04:04.275 --> 00:04:06.399
We ended up having to
write our own books and

00:04:06.399 --> 00:04:08.940
create our own resources
to support our decision.

00:04:10.710 --> 00:04:12.740
This is a huge business opportunity.

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:16.220
We're one of millions of American
parents that are no longer okay,

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.530
raising a monolingual generation.

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:19.850
American parents are looking for

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:24.100
ways to fold language learning
into their family lives.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:26.540
I myself am starting
a business in this space,

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:31.030
where we provide in home support and
guidance for parents, and a platform that

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:35.499
connects individuals with language fluency
to language learners in their communities.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:40.190
I'll never forget last summer
after one such community session,

00:04:40.190 --> 00:04:42.600
a mom coming up to me
with tears in her eyes.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.340
She said, Anna,
we've chosen to speak Spanish at home.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:47.100
But I've been worried for

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:50.770
years that my daughter would just
associate the language narrowly with us,

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:56.130
with our family life, that my daughter
wouldn't see its larger place in society.

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:58.700
Getting to come to this outdoor session,

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.120
how's my daughter see its
larger place of belonging?

00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:05.350
Her words meant a lot to me because
that was the goal of what we were doing.

00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:10.120
American businesses have an incredible
opportunity to create space for

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.490
other languages to belong
in our society and culture.

00:05:13.870 --> 00:05:16.300
My second recommendation is that

00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:20.710
American adults take up a practice
of lifelong language learning.

00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:22.400
Now, I know what you're thinking.

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:24.310
I'm just bad at languages.

00:05:24.310 --> 00:05:26.640
Most American adults feel that way.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:30.560
But the truth is American
language education is broken.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:34.680
Languages shouldn't be learned in
classrooms through reading and writing.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:36.280
Languages need to be lived.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:39.970
Languages should be acquired
through hearing and speaking.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:43.004
So it's not that you're bad at languages,

00:05:43.004 --> 00:05:46.205
you likely just haven't
had a fair shot at it.

00:05:46.205 --> 00:05:50.422
The second thing I bet many of you
are thinking is what my husband said,

00:05:50.422 --> 00:05:51.480
I'm too old.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:52.640
I'm an adult.

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:57.153
Don't studies show that if you don't pick
it up as a kid, you have no chance at it?

00:05:57.153 --> 00:06:01.203
The truth is that learning a language
early in life gives you the benefit of

00:06:01.203 --> 00:06:03.816
speaking the language
in native proficiency,

00:06:03.816 --> 00:06:07.200
which simply means you
won't have an accent.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.170
But there's nothing wrong with accents.

00:06:09.170 --> 00:06:10.810
Accents are beautiful.

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:14.930
Accents are our proof that it's never
too late to learn another language.

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:17.780
So let's remove this stigma around
accents in the United States so

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:21.050
that more American adults are willing
to engage in language education.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:24.510
I learned Arabic as an adult.

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:29.097
And though I can't tell you it is easy,
I can tell you it was incredibly enriching

00:06:29.097 --> 00:06:33.703
and something that continues to, that I
continue to be grateful for to this day.

00:06:35.709 --> 00:06:40.918
My third recommendation is that
American parents or future parents,

00:06:40.918 --> 00:06:45.580
make space in your homes for
family language learning.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:46.370
Do it together.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:50.330
Kids look at parents for cues.

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:53.424
And one of the best pieces of
parenting advice I received

00:06:53.424 --> 00:06:56.940
is that your kids will do what they see
you do, not what you tell them to do.

00:06:58.690 --> 00:07:00.900
Make space in your homes for
language learning,

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:05.330
devote an hour a week to speaking to
one another in that target language.

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:08.518
Maybe your kid's in an immersion program,
bridge that learning back home.

00:07:08.518 --> 00:07:12.420
Maybe your kid's in middle school,
in learning a new language,

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:17.120
surprise them with a phrase
that you're learning.

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:20.700
You are or can be your kid's safe space.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:22.340
Do language learning together.

00:07:22.340 --> 00:07:25.437
It will make a really meaningful impact
for your kids and our future generations.

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:30.800
Is it possible for the United States
to become more bilingual and

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:32.050
is it worth the effort?

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:37.080
My husband speaks really
good Spanish today.

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:41.100
And he has this renewed relationship
with his grandparents that's priceless.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:45.900
My son is turning five this month
which I can hardly believe.

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:49.289
And the other day he ran up to me and
said, Mommy,

00:07:49.289 --> 00:07:53.768
Mommy, let's sing the Arabic
number song and he kicked us off.

00:07:53.768 --> 00:08:03.396
[FOREIGN]

00:08:03.396 --> 00:08:03.961
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:08:03.961 --> 00:08:05.462
&gt;&gt; He loves that song.

00:08:05.462 --> 00:08:08.655
And I love that teaching it to him
has led to this deeper interest.

00:08:08.655 --> 00:08:10.990
He wants to know how other
things are said in Arabic.

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:12.800
He's fluent in English and Spanish.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:14.700
He's learning sign language in school.

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:20.350
And perhaps most importantly, he truly
believes he can learn any language.

00:08:22.140 --> 00:08:27.270
It is possible to raise a generation
of Americans that embrace bilingualism.

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:30.127
And trust me, it's so, so worth it.

00:08:30.127 --> 00:08:32.286
Thank you.

00:08:32.286 --> 00:08:35.140
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:35.140 --> 00:08:37.942
[MUSIC]

